[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 1:30] Great, let me play that back to make sure I've got the shape of it. Brew & Bean, 200 specialty coffee locations, national footprint. Over two years net margin has compressed from 24% to 6% — that's an 18-point collapse — while topline has actually grown 15%. So this isn't a demand problem, it's almost certainly a cost or mix problem, and the fact that revenue grew while margin fell that hard tells me costs grew substantially faster than revenue. Roughly, if revenue is indexed at 100 growing to 115, profit goes from 24 to about 6.9 — so absolute profit dropped more than 70% even on a bigger base. That's dramatic.

Here's how I'd structure it. First, I'd decompose profit into revenue and cost. On revenue, I want to split price versus volume versus mix — are they growing through new store openings or same-store sales? My instinct, and I'd want to confirm, is that a chain this size at 200 units is in an aggressive expansion phase, so a lot of that 15% is new-unit revenue rather than same-store traffic, which matters enormously because new units carry pre-opening and ramp costs.

Second, on cost, I'd split fixed versus variable: COGS — green coffee, dairy, cups — then labor, then occupancy and rent, then corporate overhead and marketing.

Third, external context: commodity coffee prices, wage inflation, competitive intensity.

Could I start by getting the revenue split — same-store versus new-store growth — and then the cost breakdown by line item across the two years?
